WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.599 --> 00:00:05.120
As any good tribute of Suzanne Collins' The
Hunger Games knows, the film series took a

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:08.650
few creative liberties in adapting the novels
to the big screen.

00:00:08.650 --> 00:00:12.430
From adding additional scenes to removing
characters — and whatever was happening

00:00:12.430 --> 00:00:16.840
with Seneca Crane's beard — the movies were
definitely distinct from their source material,

00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:18.520
for better and for worse.

00:00:18.520 --> 00:00:22.340
One of the subtler shifts came in the form
of the characters' looks, which tended to

00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:24.800
differ from their literary descriptions.

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:27.440
Here's how the The Hunger Games cast should
really look.

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:31.540
"This is what they do and we must fight back."

00:00:31.940 --> 00:00:32.940
Katniss Everdeen

00:00:33.279 --> 00:00:37.980
Jennifer Lawrence's version of Katniss Everdeen
sported an ornate braid to manage her long,

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:43.899
brown hair, and she had humble clothing indicative
of her life in one of Panem's less favored

00:00:43.899 --> 00:00:44.899
districts.

00:00:44.899 --> 00:00:47.979
She also had greenish-blue eyes and didn't
exactly look like she'd been on the brink

00:00:47.979 --> 00:00:49.050
of starvation.

00:00:49.050 --> 00:00:53.100
And while her ability to wield a bow and fight
for her dinner was accurate, there were still

00:00:53.100 --> 00:00:55.639
some differences to be found with her appearance.

00:00:55.639 --> 00:01:00.530
In the books, the Girl on Fire was described
as having grey eyes and being much shorter

00:01:00.530 --> 00:01:02.829
and more gaunt than her screen counterpart.

00:01:02.829 --> 00:01:07.710
But since J-Law's notorious love of french
fries is such a big part of why she's so awesome,

00:01:07.710 --> 00:01:11.060
no one seemed to care that she wasn't skin
and bones for the role, even if the movie

00:01:11.060 --> 00:01:13.030
was called The Hunger Games.

00:01:13.030 --> 00:01:14.590
Peeta Mellark

00:01:14.590 --> 00:01:18.609
The gamemakers — er, filmmakers — got
a lot of things right when it came to bringing

00:01:18.609 --> 00:01:22.050
the Boy with the Bread to cinematic life with
actor Josh Hutcherson.

00:01:22.050 --> 00:01:25.000
But they also missed the mark on a few important
points as well.

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:29.840
In the movies, Peeta has the same dishwater
blonde hair, with a medium height and a stocky

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:34.000
build earned the hard way by hauling flour
around his parents' bakery.

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:38.609
He also had hazel peepers and walked around
without any serious injury … well until

00:01:38.609 --> 00:01:40.289
the end of Catching Fire anyway.

00:01:41.660 --> 00:01:42.160
"Peeta!

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:42.660
NO!"

00:01:43.630 --> 00:01:48.400
In the books, though, Peeta lost his leg in
the first Games and had blue eyes, with wavier

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:49.400
hair.

00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:54.280
These are just minor differences, but for
the Team Peeta faithful, they were still significant.

00:01:54.280 --> 00:01:55.399
Gale Hawthorne

00:01:55.399 --> 00:01:59.550
If there's one character who was well-matched
with his actor counterpart's looks, it was

00:01:59.550 --> 00:02:03.490
Liam Hemsworth as District 12's handiest hunk,
Gale Hawthorne.

00:02:03.490 --> 00:02:08.149
In the films, he's a tall, alluringly handsome,
and strong gentleman with the same dark hair

00:02:08.149 --> 00:02:12.750
and grey eyes that mark so many of the coal
miners' children, and he could totally pass

00:02:12.750 --> 00:02:13.860
for Katniss' cousin.

00:02:13.860 --> 00:02:17.300
But there were still a few leaps from the
source material in his depiction.

00:02:17.300 --> 00:02:21.420
The book version of the character was much
more olive-toned, young, and lean from so

00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:23.320
many missed meals.

00:02:23.320 --> 00:02:24.320
Cinna

00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:27.580
The filmmakers stuck with Lenny Kravitz's
natural chocolate brown eyes for his turn

00:02:27.580 --> 00:02:32.290
as Cinna and opted to add in some facial hair
and earrings to round out his looks.

00:02:32.290 --> 00:02:37.020
Plus, he rocked some low-key fashions and
steered clear of the Capitol makeup selections,

00:02:37.020 --> 00:02:38.800
save for some gold eyeliner.

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:43.320
In the books, though, Katniss' fabulous stylist
was described as having green eyes with flecks

00:02:43.320 --> 00:02:45.060
of gold scattered throughout.

00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:49.200
Even without the right eye color and a couple
of extra earrings, Kravitz embodied all the

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:53.740
swagger and self-possession that Cinna called
for — and then some — so he was a fan-favorite

00:02:53.740 --> 00:02:55.320
despite the differences.

00:02:55.320 --> 00:02:56.700
Effie Trinket

00:02:56.700 --> 00:03:01.170
This Capitol escort's flair for extreme fashion
and hair appliances certainly came through

00:03:01.170 --> 00:03:02.690
in The Hunger Games movies.

00:03:02.690 --> 00:03:06.930
However, there were still some minor deviations
from the well-described costumery and wigs

00:03:06.930 --> 00:03:09.830
Elizabeth Banks sported throughout the written
series.

00:03:09.830 --> 00:03:14.510
In The Hunger Games, her pink wig was considered
ridiculous and slid out of place, and in Catching

00:03:14.510 --> 00:03:19.040
Fire, she was said to have donned a pumpkin-colored
headdress rather than the canary situation

00:03:19.040 --> 00:03:20.400
she wore on her head.

00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:24.680
Even though she had a few outfit and hairstyle
changes, however, that was definitely Effie

00:03:24.680 --> 00:03:26.600
we saw up there on the big screen.

00:03:26.600 --> 00:03:32.590
"Happy Hunger Games and may the odds be ever
in your favor."

00:03:32.590 --> 00:03:33.760
Haymitch Abernathy

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:38.950
In the films, Haymitch was a morose, disheveled
alcoholic who disgusted Katniss with his lack

00:03:38.950 --> 00:03:39.950
of composure.

00:03:39.950 --> 00:03:44.220
He also had straight blond locks and blue
eyes and never stumbled and fumbled his way

00:03:44.220 --> 00:03:45.910
through life in Victor Village.

00:03:45.910 --> 00:03:50.660
In the books, however, Haymitch was said to
have dark curly hair with the same grey eyes

00:03:50.660 --> 00:03:52.510
as his fellow coal district alums.

00:03:52.510 --> 00:03:55.890
It was pretty easy to forget that the hair
and eye colors were off because of how convincing

00:03:55.890 --> 00:03:59.900
Harrelson was as the mentor who decided to
shape up his act enough to help his girl "stay

00:03:59.900 --> 00:04:01.460
alive" in the arena.

00:04:01.460 --> 00:04:05.810
But for the nitpickers out there, the casting
choice was still a confusing one.

00:04:05.810 --> 00:04:06.900
Primrose Everdeen

00:04:06.900 --> 00:04:10.730
Willow Shields' turn as the little sister
who accidentally started a revolution was

00:04:10.730 --> 00:04:11.840
convincing enough.

00:04:11.840 --> 00:04:15.330
She was slight and sweet and believable as
the girl you'd go to bat for.

00:04:15.330 --> 00:04:19.430
However, in the books she was said to have
crystal blue eyes and weighed no more than

00:04:19.430 --> 00:04:23.000
"seventy pounds soaking wet," which was not
the case on-screen.

00:04:23.000 --> 00:04:24.260
President Snow

00:04:24.260 --> 00:04:28.700
Donald Sutherland's screen turn as President
Snow was one of the biggest changes from the

00:04:28.700 --> 00:04:33.510
written origins because, while he was menacing
enough with his refined sense of evil, he

00:04:33.510 --> 00:04:37.760
didn't match the book's description of the
small, thin, sinister-looking villain of the

00:04:37.760 --> 00:04:42.640
books, with a botched enhancement job on his
lips, and snake's eyes to match the unsettling

00:04:42.640 --> 00:04:43.640
appearance.

00:04:43.640 --> 00:04:47.210
Hey, at least they got the white rose on his
lapel right — of course, that'd be a hard

00:04:47.210 --> 00:04:48.430
one to screw up.

00:04:48.430 --> 00:04:49.430
Finnick Odair

00:04:49.430 --> 00:04:53.960
Whether or not Sam Claflin's version of Finnick
Odair matched the extraordinarily good-looking

00:04:53.960 --> 00:04:58.640
muscleman he was described to be is a matter
of subjective perspective, but the filmmakers

00:04:58.640 --> 00:05:01.860
certainly gave his frame some consideration
when casting the role.

00:05:01.860 --> 00:05:06.470
Not only is he athletic, tan, and cocky, but
he's also adequately redeemed by his penchant

00:05:06.470 --> 00:05:08.860
for both shirtlessness and sugar cubes on-screen.

00:05:08.860 --> 00:05:12.770
Plus, he clung to his trusty trident as tightly
as he was supposed to.

00:05:12.770 --> 00:05:14.910
The most noticeable difference from the books?

00:05:14.910 --> 00:05:19.120
The fact that his sea-green eyes weren't as
striking onscreen, and his hair was more sandy

00:05:19.120 --> 00:05:21.490
blonde than bronze, as written.

00:05:21.490 --> 00:05:22.600
Johanna Mason

00:05:22.600 --> 00:05:27.240
Personality-wise, Jena Malone's screen edition
of Johanna Mason was a dead ringer for the

00:05:27.240 --> 00:05:30.880
sassy, sharp-tongued girl we grew to know
and love in the books.

00:05:30.880 --> 00:05:33.450
“Ha, well yes, I’m angry.

00:05:33.450 --> 00:05:35.240
You know I’m getting totally screwed over
here.”

00:05:35.240 --> 00:05:36.240
“Uh huh.”

00:05:36.240 --> 00:05:40.200
“The deal was that if I win the Hunger Games,
I get to live the rest of my life in peace.”

00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:44.480
But as accurate as they got her character,
her appearance in the films was a little different.

00:05:44.480 --> 00:05:48.230
On-screen, she was lean and traditionally
pretty with long brown locks — well, until

00:05:48.230 --> 00:05:49.710
they were shaved off by the Capitol.

00:05:49.710 --> 00:05:53.900
In the books, meanwhile, her eyes were said
to be wide, while her hair was worn with a

00:05:53.900 --> 00:05:54.900
signature spike.

00:05:54.900 --> 00:05:58.330
She was also perceived by readers as being
more muscular, seeing as she was from the

00:05:58.330 --> 00:06:00.110
lumberjack district and all.

00:06:00.110 --> 00:06:01.389
President Alma Coin

00:06:01.389 --> 00:06:05.840
Julianne Moore's screen version of President
Coin was pretty faithful to the books, in

00:06:05.840 --> 00:06:09.760
that she was a middle-aged woman with wig-like
gray hair and a colorless pallor.

00:06:09.760 --> 00:06:14.620
However, she looked like she was in her late
30s and just wore a gray wig to age herself,

00:06:14.620 --> 00:06:18.850
while the book version pegged her for around
50 and with her silvery eye color being a

00:06:18.850 --> 00:06:20.370
lot more distinct.

00:06:20.370 --> 00:06:21.370
Foxface

00:06:21.370 --> 00:06:25.860
In the Hunger Games movie, Jacqueline Emerson's
vision of Foxface had bright red hair and

00:06:25.860 --> 00:06:30.820
an aptitude for stealth and low-key survivalism,
but she's hardly meek, and she certainly couldn't

00:06:30.820 --> 00:06:32.200
be confused for an animal.

00:06:32.200 --> 00:06:36.590
In the book, however, she was described as
having a face which had, well, fox-like features

00:06:36.590 --> 00:06:38.620
— hence Katniss's name for her.

00:06:38.620 --> 00:06:42.490
Anyone can open a bottle of red hair dye and
say they're Foxface, but to look the part

00:06:42.490 --> 00:06:44.350
is something else entirely.

00:06:44.350 --> 00:06:45.350
Thresh

00:06:45.350 --> 00:06:49.340
If Thresh had appeared as imposing as he was
written, there's no way audiences would believe

00:06:49.340 --> 00:06:54.060
he'd been killed in one-on-one combat with
Cato, even if the latter was a career tribute.

00:06:54.060 --> 00:06:56.360
“I’m prepared, vicious, I’m ready to
go.”

00:06:57.600 --> 00:06:58.600
“Cato!”

00:06:59.400 --> 00:07:02.960
As portrayed played by Dayo Okeniyi in the
movie, he was strong and tough, but he was

00:07:02.960 --> 00:07:07.300
also rather approachable in size and demeanor,
which made his decision to forego killing

00:07:07.300 --> 00:07:10.419
Katniss when he had the opportunity that much
more believable.

00:07:10.419 --> 00:07:14.639
Meanwhile, in the book, he's described as
having golden eyes, standing well over six

00:07:14.639 --> 00:07:20.790
feet tall, and sporting the build of an ox
from so many days in the agriculture sector.

00:07:20.790 --> 00:07:21.790
Rue

00:07:21.790 --> 00:07:22.790
Aw, Rue.

00:07:22.790 --> 00:07:25.460
A vocal contingent of Hunger Games readers
were up in arms about the fact that Rue, as

00:07:25.460 --> 00:07:29.900
played by Amandla Stenberg, was portrayed
as having brown skin in the the first film.

00:07:29.900 --> 00:07:32.800
Of course, those fans clearly misunderstood
the source material.

00:07:32.800 --> 00:07:38.090
While Rue was said to resemble Katniss's sister
Prim in size and innocence, she was specifically

00:07:38.090 --> 00:07:39.710
described as being black in the book.

00:07:39.710 --> 00:07:44.070
The film's representation of the tiny, feisty,
and quick-on-her-feet pre-teen was accurate

00:07:44.070 --> 00:07:48.650
to the writing and gave her that same smile
that affected Katniss so in both versions.

00:07:48.650 --> 00:07:52.449
She wasn't quite as frail as she was on paper,
but that's about the only major difference

00:07:52.449 --> 00:07:53.449
to be found.

00:07:53.449 --> 00:07:55.129
Otherwise, she was perfect.

00:08:01.220 --> 00:08:02.500
Thanks for watching!

00:08:02.500 --> 00:08:04.560
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:08:04.570 --> 00:08:06.840
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

